{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players","id":860359,"title":"Persuasion on players","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-06T09:16:32-07:00","id":860359,"title":"Persuasion on players","content":"Hi, how can I handle a particulary charming and persuasive NPC (or other PC), who is tryng to convince a PC about something? For instance the NPC is tryng to pull the PC on his side while he is working for the opposing faction.<br /> I thought to let the player who won't fallow the desires of the NPC to roll resolve+composure - successes achieved in the persuasion roll.<br /> Is the doors mechanic applicable in this kind of situation?<br /> How do you handle these situations?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:26:40-07:00","id":860366,"title":"","content":"Roll it, see if the NPC wins in social maneuvering, and if they do, ask the PC to choose between going along with it or instead taking a custom Condition. I say custom because for this type of thing I'd want something with some real bite to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:40:23-07:00","id":860375,"title":"","content":"You can easily use the Doors mechanic for it. Calculate accordingly. If you want to be generous to the PC, allow them to spend Willpower to prevent a Door from being opened or forced (on a 1 for 1 basis), or follow the old Mirrors suggestion and give them 1 Beat for every Door opened.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:40:46-07:00","id":860376,"title":"","content":"Mmm what kind of custom condition? Can you tell me some example?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:50:17-07:00","id":860383,"title":"","content":"I suggest going for Inspired. Not only do they benefit from bonuses while -doing- the project, but they get beats for it. Inspired is my go-to Condition for encouraging PCs to do something.<br /> <br /> You can also be sneaky and offer them a new Aspiration to go along with the NPC's suggestion - either replacing an old one, or a temporary fourth one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T09:53:13-07:00","id":860385,"title":"","content":"Thank you all <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T10:45:29-07:00","id":860428,"title":"","content":"This is kind of what the Leveraged Condition is meant to simulate, as an aside.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T10:47:58-07:00","id":860430,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's what I typically use for Social Maneuvering on PCs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T11:07:49-07:00","id":860437,"title":"","content":"Personally, I don't like to let dice decide stuff like this. Taking away player agency and choice is dangerous...<br /> <br /> If I want the players to potentially side with a character who's supposed to be charismatic/reasonable/etc, I let them interact in person and make that NPC as charismatic as possible. ..Actually, a <i>lot </i>of my antagonists have basically had a chat with the PCs, trying to get them on side. They've previously sided with an Abyssal spirit possessing a corpse and an angel (though to be fair the angel had a lot of leverage, and they <i>hated </i>that SOB), spared a Strix and walked away from a bunch of changeling Privateers. ...This makes my players sound incredibly skeezy. They turned down a lot of other monstrous beings, and in most of the above examples they didn't have all the facts.<br /> <br /> Conditions like Leveraged and Inspired are a great way to mechanically nudge PCs without actually forcing them, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":452,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","user":"Eremite","date":". 04-06-2016, 02:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T11:36:10-07:00","id":860457,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eremite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=860437#post860437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I don't like to let dice decide stuff like this. Taking away player agency and choice is dangerous...<br /> <br /> If I want the players to potentially side with a character who's supposed to be charismatic/reasonable/etc, I let them interact in person and make that NPC as charismatic as possible. ..Actually, a <i>lot </i>of my antagonists have basically had a chat with the PCs, trying to get them on side. They've previously sided with an Abyssal spirit possessing a corpse and an angel (though to be fair the angel had a lot of leverage, and they hated that SOB), spared a Strix and walked away from a bunch of changeling Privateers. ...This makes my players sound incredibly skeezy. They turned down a lot of other monstrous beings, and in most of the above examples they didn't have all the facts.<br /> <br /> Conditions like Leveraged and Inspired are a great way to mechanically nudge PCs without actually forcing them, though.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Yeah. I think if players are on board with the idea of characters being persuaded by dice, all you've got to do is say, &quot;Joe makes a convincing argument&quot; then offer them a Condition like Leveraged. If they want the Beat, they'll go along with it, and act out as if their character had been persuaded.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T11:39:32-07:00","id":860460,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eremite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=860437#post860437\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I don't like to let dice decide stuff like this. Taking away player agency and choice is dangerous...<br /> <br /> If I want the players to potentially side with a character who's supposed to be charismatic/reasonable/etc, I let them interact in person and make that NPC as charismatic as possible. ..Actually, a <i>lot </i>of my antagonists have basically had a chat with the PCs, trying to get them on side. They've previously sided with an Abyssal spirit possessing a corpse and an angel (though to be fair the angel had a lot of leverage, and they hated that SOB), spared a Strix and walked away from a bunch of changeling Privateers. ...This makes my players sound incredibly skeezy. They turned down a lot of other monstrous beings, and in most of the above examples they didn't have all the facts.<br /> <br /> Conditions like Leveraged and Inspired are a great way to mechanically nudge PCs without actually forcing them, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes but sometime, a really charismatic person (even more if he uses Majesty), can let other people do strange or illogical things that a Player, not emotionally involved, would never do.<br /> How can you let a player to betray the Prince he works for, that will give him rewards and political power, just becouse a really awesome woman try to seduce him?<br /> I think, in these rare cases or particualar story hooks, the rolls are a fair way to handle this, giving to the character the chance to resist using the traits he put on his sheet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-06T12:31:42-07:00","id":860483,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=860460#post860460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yes but sometime, a really charismatic person (even more if he uses Majesty), can let other people do strange or illogical things that a Player, not emotionally involved, would never do.<br /> How can you let a player to betray the Prince he works for, that will give him rewards and political power, just becouse a really awesome woman try to seduce him?<br /> I think, in these rare cases or particualar story hooks, the rolls are a fair way to handle this, giving to the character the chance to resist using the traits he put on his sheet.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Majesty especially is the key thing, I think. Majesty is a really fun tool for players to use, but it's also a very frightening tool to use against them -- it helps to have a framework to convey how their players are slowly being supernaturally seduced into their minds being changed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1097-underwoodfive","name":"underwoodfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1097_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"47"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T07:14:08-07:00","id":861020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players#post860359\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi, how can I handle a particulary charming and persuasive NPC (or other PC), who is tryng to convince a PC about something?</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I, speaking as the NPC, could not convince the PC, I just let it go. Players hate to be forced to act in specific ways. I get the idea of using conditions to bait them into doing it, but that would be frustrating to me as well, because you're still basically forcing them, just with prizes instead of punishments. <br /> <br /> A charming NPC can charm other NPCs, and should do so, often. But PCs are special and different. They're PCs. They can choose their own actions and reactions.<br /> <br /> But yeah, I know that my opinion here is going to be the minority.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T07:46:02-07:00","id":861037,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861020#post861020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I, speaking as the NPC, could not convince the PC, I just let it go. Players hate to be forced to act in specific ways. I get the idea of using conditions to bait them into doing it, but that would be frustrating to me as well, because you're still basically forcing them, just with prizes instead of punishments.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess, in the same way that you're &quot;forced&quot; to play a carnival game if you want the prizes they offer. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861020#post861020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A charming NPC can charm other NPCs, and should do so, often. But PCs are special and different. They're PCs. They can choose their own actions and reactions.</div> </div> </div> </div>The way I see it, offering a Beat for doing what the NPC wants still gives the player agency in the matter. They can choose not to do what the NPC wants, but there is an incentive for them to do it. It's up to the player to decide if the incentive is worth the buy-in. They still get to choose their own actions and reactions, but I don't have to rely on my real life persuasion skills to make it tempting to choose to do what a persuasive NPC wants, just like I don't have to rely on my real life combat skills to make NPCs deal damage to PCs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 04-07-2016, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T08:43:08-07:00","id":861070,"title":"","content":"Exactly. This is the point. A really strong PNC could force you to do what he want with violence....persuasion is just another kind of &quot;subtle&quot; violence. In this world of darkness, corruption, love and passions (as for the mental powers), are something to deal with as for physical violence.<br /> I mean, an elder is an elder and with awe or even with his mundane Presence or Persuasion, could have a strong hold on your character.<br /> If you take Resolve and Composure 1 for your character, is normal it's easier to condition him.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T08:53:58-07:00","id":861078,"title":"","content":"I've seen really valid arguments for both sides of the issue. <br /> <br /> If you have it down to dice, you ignore the importance of the PCs that some games foster where the PCs tend to be a little better than everyone else, but also you risk an NPC or another PC just taking over the game with their dominate. But then everything is done in-game. You have your entire character sheet to work with, as does the ST, and you can pull out your bonuses and counter abilities.<br /> <br /> Just relying on role-playing, you potentially allow for metagaming, as players might not want to do what the convincing person suggests their character do, even if their character has low Resolve and mental fortitude. There's also the problem of the Storyteller, if they cannot role-play a convincing or charismatic person it can all fall flat, especially in games where you have beings that can reach inhuman levels of persuasion. It's easy to role-play a character who can punch through cars, but someone who could convince anyone to do anything with a single sentence is beyond most people's capability. However then you do have more 'role-playing' as opposed to 'roll-playing' and the players have more of a sense of agency.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T09:02:16-07:00","id":861087,"title":"","content":"Beats make unattractive options seem more attractive. That's basically what being persuasive is all about.<br /> <br /> If you want to do something quick n' dirty, make a contested roll whenever an NPC wants to convince a PC into doing something unattractive, or one PC wants to convince another PC into doing something unattractive: Attribute + Skill vs Attribute + Skill.<br /> <br /> If the player resisting wins, give them a beat for resisting the NPC's / other PC's persuasive charm. If the player resisting wins with an exceptional success, give them the Steadfast condition.<br /> <br /> If the player resisting loses, give them the Leveraged condition. If they lose and the NPC / other player scores an exceptional success, give them two conditions: Leveraged and Inspired. I would give the player trying to persuade the other the Steadfast condition on an exceptional success, too. If this is done in a public space, I would give them a variance of the Connected condition with an exceptional success on top of that.<br /> <br /> Basically, Leveraged is a carrot on a stick for any player. If you go along with what is requested, you resolve the condition and score a beat, and Inspired can add to that by rewarding the player with a Will Power point, another beat, and allow an exceptional success on a roll pertaining to whatever they've been persuaded to do with three successes instead of five. Steadfast is a great way at rewarding those with extra beats upon resolving the condition and by giving them an option to turn future failures into successes, and to make impossible situations seem more possible. The Connected condition in the context of trying to persuade and charm somebody publicly basically makes onlookers marvel at your character's charismatic prowess, and therefore gets a +2 bonus on all rolls relating to this group (simulating he's become more chummy), or resolve it to score a beat and get a one-off +3 instead.<br /> <br /> HOWEVER! Your players still have agency, that's important. They can still say 'no'. They aren't under any obligation to say 'yes'. But if they do, they just don't get any reward. And there's outside factors to take into account, too - that's what the Connected condition represents - if these negotiations are happening publicly, and a person still says 'no' after being trounced, they're going to potentially look foolish. And the persuasive aggressor certainly has the chance to turn the public against them with a second roll (now incurring a bonus) to make that so. However, that person might want to use that Connected roll to allow the other person to save face, trying to get in their good books instead.<br /> <br /> edit: also, don't forget modifiers. If you're wearing a crown, or the other person is in a position where they aren't going to be very inclined to say 'no', then feel free to give modifiers to the roll (-5 to +5). The way I figure it is requesting somebody to do things that personally costs them incurs negative modifiers, and requesting somebody to do things that they're being incentivized to do in-character incurs positive modifiers. The size of the modifier depends on the extremity of what's on offer or what the cost is. Modifiers could be &quot;it goes against your morals&quot;, &quot;you could be punished severely if you do it&quot;, &quot;it would be personally embarrassing&quot;, &quot;bribery&quot;, or &quot;a loved one has been taken hostage&quot;.<br /> <br /> On top of that, it doesn't need to just be the Inspired condition that you give players. You might want to replace the Inspired condition on an exceptional success with something more contextually appropriate: perhaps the Swooning condition (like Marcus suggests below, seduction as a means of persuasion), Guilty condition (making the person feel bad until they do what you want), the Notoriety condition (you make them look awful in front of everyone), the Distracted condition (you've managed to fast-talk your way into distracting and confusing them, in fact it turns out your initial persuasiveness was merely a red herring for something more sinister).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1592,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","user":"Jack","date":". 04-07-2016, 12:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T09:08:09-07:00","id":861089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861078#post861078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It's easy to role-play a character who can punch through cars, but someone who could convince anyone to do anything with a single sentence is beyond most people's capability.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also because the apparence (Presence) of the persuader is an important factor. <br /> You know, a beautiful girl is able to obtain a lot just with his smile and a few words, not only with a well argued speech. If she talks to a low resolve boys at least <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 04-07-2016, 12:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T18:14:30-07:00","id":861480,"title":"","content":"Honestly people have given great arguments on both sides with regards to persuading players. Honestly my suggestion would be to talk to your players and find out what they're cool with. I've had players who are 100% fine going along with being socially maneuvered and those for whom losing agency truly ruins their play experience. If you've players in the latter camp then I would suggest respecting their wishes there as anything cool that might come of their character being persuaded will be lost by them not enjoying it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2989-violetdea","name":"violetdea","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-07T23:08:02-07:00","id":861624,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861037#post861037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess, in the same way that you're &quot;forced&quot; to play a carnival game if you want the prizes they offer.</div> </div> </div> </div>The real problem is because you're tantalizing them with an in game prize, you're tantalizing them with a meta game prize. It's not &quot;Do this thing your character wouldn't do because of a thing they character can directly connect to what they want.&quot; It's &quot;make your character do this thing they wouldn't otherwise do in order to magically learn 1/15 of a new super power.&quot; We kind of went through this before, though, so, we can just agree to disagree, that's ok.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861037#post861037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">but I don't have to rely on my real life persuasion skills to make it tempting to choose to do what a persuasive NPC wants, just like I don't have to rely on my real life combat skills to make NPCs deal damage to PCs.</div> </div> </div> </div>But, PCs do rely on their tactical knowledge to make smart moves in combats and deal damage. And they rely on their intelligence and ability to parse the system and spend their points/XP wisely to make the most effective characters. Real life skills leak into the game all over the place, so, objecting to this specific one (i.e. being persuasive) is always strange to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T00:42:25-07:00","id":861662,"title":"","content":"There is no one true way to doing it, so if that's your philosophy at the table, that's that.<br /> <br /> That said, I personally try to roleplay even when making those combat decisions. I try not to just think about what is the most mechanically efficient, but also what's in-character. For example, despite it being unwise to do so, I tend to make a lot of all-out attacks on characters who aren't that combat savvy. What that represents to me is that the vast majority of people don't really know how to defend themselves when they actually try to hurt other people. It's little stuff like that which you can do to further evince your character in combat and not just be focused on &quot;winning&quot; the encounter.<br /> <br /> But I think there's a more fundamental disconnect here. I sincerely doubt anyone is objecting to people &quot;allowing real life skills to leak into the game.&quot; But rather protesting mismatched expectations. If your ST shows up expecting people to do what's interesting and people only do the thing that gets them the most dice while ignoring things that complicate them, that's just a case of mismatched expectations. It's not wrong for the players to not be interested, but it's definitely something the group needs to feel out beforehand.<br /> <br /> For those who don't mind surrendering their character to the dice, or going with where the roleplay leads versus choosing not to do something because you think it would end badly as a player, then for those people maybe the dice will inject a bit of randomness to enjoy themselves. Maybe it doesn't work for everybody, but I do suggest people give this style of playing a shot before categorically refusing stuff like social checks and others.<br /> <br /> Plus, it stands to reason that <i>CofD </i>would try to push this way of playing. This mindset is pretty vital to players utilizing and playing with Conditions in the way were you get the most mileage out of them. Why would taking the Leveraged Condition be any different? It encourages you with Beats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T09:08:08-07:00","id":861878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861624#post861624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The real problem is because you're tantalizing them with an in game prize, you're tantalizing them with a meta game prize. It's not &quot;Do this thing your character wouldn't do because of a thing they character can directly connect to what they want.&quot; It's &quot;make your character do this thing they wouldn't otherwise do in order to magically learn 1/15 of a new super power.&quot; We kind of went through this before, though, so, we can just agree to disagree, that's ok.</div> </div> </div> </div>The player decides what their character would or would not do. If the thing being asked is something your character wouldn't do, don't do it. The Beat encourages you to consider the possibility that your character might do something you as a player wouldn't want them to do, and why they might be willing to do it. I get it though, that's &quot;director stance&quot; thinking, and you prefer players to always be in &quot;actor stance&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861624#post861624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But, PCs do rely on their tactical knowledge to make smart moves in combats and deal damage. And they rely on their intelligence and ability to parse the system and spend their points/XP wisely to make the most effective characters. Real life skills leak into the game all over the place, so, objecting to this specific one (i.e. being persuasive) is always strange to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because I can easily make a character (whether PC or NPC) that is more persuasive than I can realistically portray. If a character who's supposed to be at the pinnacle of human ability when it comes to natural charisma and persuasive skills can't convince a character that has less than average social and mental fortitude just because I as an ST am just average at persuading people and my players aren't easily swayed, that's both verisimilitude breaking and system breaking.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 04-08-2016, 01:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T09:55:35-07:00","id":861923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861624#post861624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's &quot;make your character do this thing they wouldn't otherwise do in order to magically learn 1/15 of a new super power.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Beats are not a fraction of a power or an attribute or a skill or whatever. Beats represent the lessons your character has learnt and the inner power they have accrued. When a character is swayed by another and they gain a Beat for going along with it, they are learning a lesson, or being hardened/forged/empowered by the hardship or experience they went through.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:32:51-07:00","id":861952,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861878#post861878\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because I can easily make a character (whether PC or NPC) that is more persuasive than I can realistically portray. If a character who's supposed to be at the pinnacle of human ability when it comes to natural charisma and persuasive skills can't convince a character that has less than average social and mental fortitude just because I as an ST am just average at persuading people and my players aren't easily swayed, that's both verisimilitude breaking and system breaking.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know, I think I am as persuasive as I am precisely because I've roleplayed for more than 20 years. It's a thing you become, doing it enough. <br /> <br /> I was trying to come up with an example for how it works, but, I actually couldn't think of any. I realized I just, subconsciously, I guess, avoid having NPCs try to persuade PCs to specific courses of action. The most they do, that would require a roll, is lie. If it ever did come up, though, I would like to think I would just say something like, &quot;Well, she got 5 successes to persuade you on this. How do you respond?&quot; And then, just trust them to interpret that as would fit their character. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowKnight1224</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861923#post861923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Beats are not a fraction of a power or an attribute or a skill or whatever. Beats represent the lessons your character has learnt and the inner power they have accrued. When a character is swayed by another and they gain a Beat for going along with it, they are learning a lesson, or being hardened/forged/empowered by the hardship or experience they went through.</div> </div> </div> </div>...and those lessons translate directly into super powers. If 15 people convince my Gangrel of shit and I go along with it each time, what I learned from going along with people is...how to shapeshift?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:38:11-07:00","id":861954,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...and those lessons translate directly into super powers. If 15 people convince my Gangrel of shit and I go along with it each time, what I learned from going along with people is...how to shapeshift?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure. You learned to become the tool of other people, your body learns to be a tool for your own ends.<br /> <br /> Presumably your gangrel has learned something from doing things, they've had experiences that have changed them for one way or another.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:40:41-07:00","id":861956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ShadowKnight1224</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861923#post861923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Beats are not a fraction of a power or an attribute or a skill or whatever. Beats represent the lessons your character has learnt and the inner power they have accrued. When a character is swayed by another and they gain a Beat for going along with it, they are learning a lesson, or being hardened/forged/empowered by the hardship or experience they went through.</div> </div> </div> </div>I totally agree. You can spent that beat (in future) to rise resolve or composure...not just to buy new powers. And this is the real meaning of the beats point. You face a trouble? You learn how to avoid or fix it in the future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:43:39-07:00","id":861958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...and those lessons translate directly into super powers. If 15 people convince my Gangrel of shit and I go along with it each time, what I learned from going along with people is...how to shapeshift?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not necessarily. Those lessons translate into whatever the character grows into. Maybe he realises that he's a pawn in everyone's schemes and raises Composure and Resolve, or buys the Closed Book merit. Maybe he gives in, accepts his role, and starts training hard into what is expected of him. He internalises the manipulation and the scheming, and chooses to become the weapon they want him to be. He learns to shapeshift.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861956#post861956\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I totally agree. You can spent that beat (in future) to rise resolve or composure...not just to buy new powers. And this is the real meaning of the beats point. You face a trouble? You learn how to avoid or fix it in the future.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, and maybe you are so impressed that instead you start thinking about doing the same, and you end up raising Presence, Manipulation, or getting Majesty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T10:46:43-07:00","id":861961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know, I think I am as persuasive as I am precisely because I've roleplayed for more than 20 years. It's a thing you become, doing it enough. <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>You, as ST, are more persuasive then a hypotetichal vampire with persuasion 5 and manipulation 5 (and maybe Majesty)? Wow...XD","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:02:52-07:00","id":861976,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know, I think I am as persuasive as I am precisely because I've roleplayed for more than 20 years. It's a thing you become, doing it enough.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm pretty good at portraying most characters, but I don't think I'm at the peak of human ability at any attributes or skills, let alone superhuman. This isn't a problem with physical attributes and skills because the dice do the punching for me. But if I can't convince a PC to my way of thinking, even though the NPC has 7 Manipulation and Persuasion 4 as an Asset Skill with two relevant Area of Expertise specialties, that both breaks verisimilitude and makes all those dots pretty much meaningless. That's not a real character I made, but the point is I don't want my real life social and mental skills to limit the range of NPCs I can create and portray faithfully.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was trying to come up with an example for how it works, but, I actually couldn't think of any. I realized I just, subconsciously, I guess, avoid having NPCs try to persuade PCs to specific courses of action. The most they do, that would require a roll, is lie. If it ever did come up, though, I would like to think I would just say something like, &amp;quot;Well, she got 5 successes to persuade you on this. How do you respond?&amp;quot; And then, just trust them to interpret that as would fit their character.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's basically what I'm advocating for. The thing is, I want players to feel encouraged to play characters who would respond to such situations in ways that might not always be beneficial for them as a player. The Beat is the consolation prize that makes it feel worthwhile to play a character who makes bad decisions sometimes.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861952#post861952\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...and those lessons translate directly into super powers. If 15 people convince my Gangrel of shit and I go along with it each time, what I learned from going along with people is...how to shapeshift?</div> </div> </div> </div>You can't just buy anything you want simply because you have the experiences for it. The purchase has to be justified within the narrative. Now, in this case I agree with Nofather's interpretation of why this would be a justified buy, but if I didn't, I would be within my rights as ST to say that the player can't make that purchase.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:27:56-07:00","id":862001,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861961#post861961\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You, as ST, are more persuasive then a hypotetichal vampire with persuasion 5 and manipulation 5 (and maybe Majesty)? Wow...XD</div> </div> </div> </div>You know the old saying that you don't have to outrun the bear, just the guy next to you? I don't have to be 10 dice persuasive. I just have to be persuasive enough to appear convincing. That, combined with all the NPCs falling all over the character in question, it should portray the 10 dice just fine.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861976#post861976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But if I can't convince a PC to my way of thinking, even though the NPC has 7 Manipulation and Persuasion 4 as an Asset Skill with two relevant Area of Expertise specialties, that both breaks verisimilitude and makes all those dots pretty much meaningless.</div> </div> </div> </div>A Manipulation 7 vampire is a BP 7+ elder who obviously has way better &quot;forcing you to do it anyway&quot; super powers anyway. I don't think I would ever portray such a person resorting to mundane persuasion anyway. Why would he bother? But ok, not taking that at face value, taking that as the concept of a character beyond your capability to play, I still think there are other ways around this. As I said above, I just have to be convincing enough--I don't have to exactly match the right levels, just get it in the ballpark. I find that when you immerse, you auto-translate stuff. In D&amp;D, if someone says &quot;Come at me, bro!&quot; you don't respond, &quot;Who is this bro you speak of, knave!&quot; you just translate that mentally into the jargon of the setting, the same way we don't think it's weird that two WWII Nazi officers speak English with German accents to each other when totally alone in movies. When Lord Snaketongue offers you deal, you view it in favorable light. But look, in the end, you can fast talk someone into ignoring or forgetting facts, but unless you lie, the facts of a deal are the facts, and they're not going to randomly change or whatever because the guy offering the deal is wily.<br /> <br /> If he wants you to help him, even though doing so is against your interest, there's no way that's going to fly, unless you just absolutely don't understand he circumstances or something. He would have to offer you something that is in your interest that sufficiently compensates for how put out you are by the request. The only thing he could really do to change that is lie.<br /> <br /> My problem comes when you throw the beat into the mix, because what's happening, is that his eloquence is literally creating a reward from thin air, that the player now has to consider when weighing their decision, but that the character can't really know about or interact with in any meaningful way. It's not part of the character's decision, just the player's.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861976#post861976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's basically what I'm advocating for. The thing is, I want players to feel encouraged to play characters who would respond to such situations in ways that might not always be beneficial for them as a player. The Beat is the consolation prize that makes it feel worthwhile to play a character who makes bad decisions sometimes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see that side of things, yes, but what about the other side? Where the Beat becomes the a lure to someone to break character for more power? See, the assumption here is always that the character would/should take the deal, but what about when they really wouldn't/shouldn't? There are times when it just wouldn't make any sense to agree to something, but that tantalizing Beat is going to make them do it. A potential consolation prize for acting in character is not worth the risk of not acting in character for power. I'd rather just trust them to act in character and choose their actions that way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861976#post861976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can't just buy anything you want simply because you have the experiences for it. The purchase has to be justified within the narrative. Now, in this case I agree with Nofather's interpretation of why this would be a justified buy, but if I didn't, I would be within my rights as ST to say that the player can't make that purchase.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow, I think I would have a riot at the table. And frankly, if I ever PCed, I would be flipping the table, too.<br /> <br /> &quot;You can't buy Protean, you got that XP by getting leveraged in bad deals.&quot;<br /> &quot;Yeah, because you kept presenting bad deals. How, exactly, do I get XP for <i>trying to shapeshift</i> again?&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't know, I am so fascinated by how far apart my games and general roleplaying experience is from others here. Sorry if I am disrupting things by talking about it so much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:41:55-07:00","id":862018,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">his eloquence is literally creating a reward from thin air, that the player now has to consider when weighing their decision, but that the character can't really know about or interact with in any meaningful way.</div> </div> </div> </div>The reward is not being created out of thin air. The reward comes from the fact that this persuasion is <i>affecting the character on a significant level</i>. That's where the Beat is coming from. Conditions give you Beats all the time, because they represent emotional, physical, mental or supernatural states that are having a significant influence on the character. Addicted doesn't generate a reward out of thin air when the character gives up an obligation to get another hit, it gives a reward because such an act had a significant impact on the character, enough to teach him a lesson or to slowly add to the inner experiences and power of the character, so that later on he can say &quot;my experiences with drug use have hardened me against this [Takes another dot of Stamina].&quot;<br /> <br /> Likewise, social eloquence has an impact on the character if the character goes along with it, <i>precisely because the character is being influenced to do something they might not have wanted to do in the first place</i>. This has a transformative effect on the character, albeit a tiny one in the grand scheme of things.<br /> <br /> Your particular problem with understanding these mechanics is twofold. First, you are dismissive of &quot;meta&quot; mechanics, and instead of allowing them to slot into play naturally, you resist them, creating these exact cases where people tell you repeatedly how this makes absolutely perfect sense, but for you it makes none whatsoever, since you have pre-emptively driven a wedge between meta mechanics and in-game situations. Secondly, you underuse mechanics in favour of freeforming, which lends itself to you not having the tools you need to further incorporate these meta mechanics.<br /> <br /> In short, you have created a situation through your years of roleplaying where these exact mechanics seem extremely foreign to you because you have pre-emptively rejected the tools you need to properly interface them with their corresponding in-game situations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:51:56-07:00","id":862035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know the old saying that you don't have to outrun the bear, just the guy next to you? I don't have to be 10 dice persuasive. I just have to be persuasive enough to appear convincing. That, combined with all the NPCs falling all over the character in question, it should portray the 10 dice just fine.</div> </div> </div> </div>That works when you do manage to convince the player despite not actually being as persuasive as the character. It doesn't work if you fail, because, not being as persuasive as the character, you don't know if someone more persuasive might have been able to make a more convincing argument that might have swayed them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Manipulation 7 vampire is a BP 7+ elder who obviously has way better &amp;quot;forcing you to do it anyway&amp;quot; super powers anyway. I don't think I would ever portray such a person resorting to mundane persuasion anyway. Why would he bother? But ok, not taking that at face value, taking that as the concept of a character beyond your capability to play, I still think there are other ways around this. As I said above, I just have to be convincing enough--I don't have to exactly match the right levels, just get it in the ballpark. I find that when you immerse, you auto-translate stuff. In D&amp;D, if someone says &amp;quot;Come at me, bro!&amp;quot; you don't respond, &amp;quot;Who is this bro you speak of, knave!&amp;quot; you just translate that mentally into the jargon of the setting, the same way we don't think it's weird that two WWII Nazi officers speak English with German accents to each other when totally alone in movies. When Lord Snaketongue offers you deal, you view it in favorable light. But look, in the end, you can fast talk someone into ignoring or forgetting facts, but unless you lie, the facts of a deal are the facts, and they're not going to randomly change or whatever because the guy offering the deal is wily.<br /> <br /> If he wants you to help him, even though doing so is against your interest, there's no way that's going to fly, unless you just absolutely don't understand he circumstances or something. He would have to offer you something that is in your interest that sufficiently compensates for how put out you are by the request. The only thing he could really do to change that is lie.</div> </div> </div> </div>Half-truths and spin are the bread and butter of persuasion, and not being preternaturally persuasive myself, I will never know if there might have been a more appealing spin I could have put on the facts or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My problem comes when you throw the beat into the mix, because what's happening, is that his eloquence is literally creating a reward from thin air, that the player now has to consider when weighing their decision, but that the character can't really know about or interact with in any meaningful way. It's not part of the character's decision, just the player's.<br /> <br /> I see that side of things, yes, but what about the other side? Where the Beat becomes the a lure to someone to break character for more power? See, the assumption here is always that the character would/should take the deal, but what about when they really wouldn't/shouldn't? There are times when it just wouldn't make any sense to agree to something, but that tantalizing Beat is going to make them do it. A potential consolation prize for acting in character is not worth the risk of not acting in character for power. I'd rather just trust them to act in character and choose their actions that way.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is where we get into that &quot;director stance&quot; vs. &quot;actor stance&quot; thing that I mentioned earlier. The way I see it, there's no such thing as an out-of-character action. The player determines what the character would or would not do, so what ever action the player decides to have the character take, that is by definition the action the character would have taken. Players decide what actions their characters would take based on out of character factors <i>all the time</i>, whether they realize it or not. But some actions (usually those that benefit the character) have more out of character factors that encourage the player to portray characters that would take them, while others (usually those that are a detriment to the character) actively discourage the player from portraying characters that would take them. Beats give the player a reason to want to play a character that would take actions that might be detrimental to them. They don't force the player to do so. You offer options, and let the player make an informed decision. That decision will always be informed by meta-game factors anyway, so I don't see a problem with using meta-game factors to encourage a better story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wow, I think I would have a riot at the table. And frankly, if I ever PCed, I would be flipping the table, too.<br /> <br /> &amp;quot;You can't buy Protean, you got that XP by getting leveraged in bad deals.&amp;quot;<br /> &quot;Yeah, because you kept presenting bad deals. How, exactly, do I get XP for <i>trying to shapeshift</i> again?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>And this is why Aspirations are a thing. They allow the players to communicate to he storyteller what kinds of learning experiences they want their characters to be presented with.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know, I am so fascinated by how far apart my games and general roleplaying experience is from others here. Sorry if I am disrupting things by talking about it so much.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm enjoying the discussion as well, so no worries. If it's bothering anyone else, let us know, I wouldn't mind carrying it out over PMs or something instead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T11:55:29-07:00","id":862040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see that side of things, yes, but what about the other side? Where the Beat becomes the a lure to someone to break character for more power? See, the assumption here is always that the character would/should take the deal, but what about when they really wouldn't/shouldn't? There are times when it just wouldn't make any sense to agree to something, but that tantalizing Beat is going to make them do it. A potential consolation prize for acting in character is not worth the risk of not acting in character for power. I'd rather just trust them to act in character and choose their actions that way.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you trust your players to make the right decision to not 'violate' the authenticity of their character in general, then I don't understand why you don't trust them to respond to XP incentives similarly. You can say it isn't necessary because your players are already doing that automatically, but you shouldn't argue it both ways. <br /> <br /> Beats are also the sugar that makes the bitter pill go down easier when facing things like frenzy, hard leverage, Daeva obsessions, etc - things you seem to classify as &quot;Loss Conditions&quot; that you and your players would never, ever want to experience in game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wow, I think I would have a riot at the table. And frankly, if I ever PCed, I would be flipping the table, too.<br /> <br /> &quot;You can't buy Protean, you got that XP by getting leveraged in bad deals.&quot;<br /> &quot;Yeah, because you kept presenting bad deals. How, exactly, do I get XP for <i>trying to shapeshift</i> again?&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're misconstruing here. XP expenditures have to be justified by in-game actions. Someone who takes a beat for losing an argument doesn't have a justification for learning Vigor. If they later trade a boon for training from a Daeva, the justification to use that beat for Vigor now exists.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T12:13:19-07:00","id":862065,"title":"","content":"This discussion has been really interesting and thought provoking.<br /> <br /> Since it's really had me thinking about why i do things the way I do, I've realised why I don't offer any mechanical carrots or sticks when an antagonist explains themselves and even when they attempt to get the PCs on-side is because, well, I'm genuinely interested in what they decide. I often plan in vague, or even concrete, terms, the fallout of siding with or against such a figure. And these are some of my favourite things when I'm RPGing, the moments where we all get to know who the characters <i>are</i>. Where they can surprise me and even their players. Are they merciful to a monstrous, soulless abomination that'll probably just keep hurting the world? Are they swayed by pragmatism or idealism? Justice or mercy? Who <i>are </i>the PCs? <br /> <br /> Interestingly. one situation that has come up when I <i>should</i> probably have been doing something mechanically (human PCs unknowingly meeting a Promethean), I figured mechanical trappings could give away too much. So I tried to describe even his mundane actions and mannerisms in a slightly suspicious way. The PCs, sure enough, were a little suspicious of this character. Long-term exposure would have required that mechanics get involved, but the Promethean character consciously avoided that.<br /> <br /> Note that I'm not opposed to, in future, using mechanical tricks, especially if supernatural power and actual blackmail come up. Off the top of my head, I've only used Nightmare to mess with PC heads previously.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T12:44:06-07:00","id":862094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862001#post862001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know the old saying that you don't have to outrun the bear, just the guy next to you? I don't have to be 10 dice persuasive. I just have to be persuasive enough to appear convincing. That, combined with all the NPCs falling all over the character in question, it should portray the 10 dice just fine.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but are you able to convince a player that abandon all his political power, betray the trust of the Prince, to leave with the NPC and start a new un-life, from zero, elsewehere, just describing a wide smile and the sensation of butterfles in the stomach (Hig presence+persuasion)? I don't think so, while a PNC (or other PC), with Presence 5, Persuasion 5 and Majesty 3-4, could achieve this goal.<br /> It's just an hypotetical situation but it describe something that really could happen in real life. You know, people that leave work and sell everything to satisfy a woman that then abandons him. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T12:49:47-07:00","id":862099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862094#post862094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sure, but are you able to convince a player that abandon all his political power, betray the trust of the Prince, to leave with the NPC and start a new un-life, from zero, elsewehere, just describing a wide smile and the sensation of butterfles in the stomach (Hig presence+persuasion)? I don't think so, while a PNC (or other PC), with Presence 5, Persuasion 5 and Majesty 3-4, could achieve this goal.<br /> It's just an hypotetical situation but it describe something that really could happen in real life. You know, people that leave work and sell everything to satisfy a woman that then abandons him.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, you're right, but that feels like a bad example. Doing this to a player is kinda the same as unleashing a combat monster with lots of Physical Disciplines (and the skills to use them effectively), Protean (optional Claws of the Unholy) and other nasty tricks. If the player is optimised for combat, that's a fun challenge. If they aren't, you're curb-stomping them, and that's not usually fun for the player and is generally Not Done.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T13:05:21-07:00","id":862120,"title":"","content":"It's worth keeping in mind that this Beat for going the other way isn't really a special benefit.<br /> <br /> Ideally your players and their characters are going after their own Aspirations. Them going another way derails them from that, but they can still gain Beats that they won't be getting for pursuing their Aspiration.<br /> <br /> It's also important to remember that your experience, no matter how vast you may consider it, is not the total experience of all gamers everywhere. What happens at your table, what the players bring to it, how they react to things, how you plan or don't, is going to be entirely different even from another table where they they follow the same rules. How you or your players would react to things is not how all players would react to things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 04-08-2016, 04:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T14:31:31-07:00","id":862191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eremite</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862099#post862099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I mean, you're right, but that feels like a bad example. Doing this to a player is kinda the same as unleashing a combat monster with lots of Physical Disciplines (and the skills to use them effectively), Protean (optional Claws of the Unholy) and other nasty tricks. If the player is optimised for combat, that's a fun challenge. If they aren't, you're curb-stomping them, and that's not usually fun for the player and is generally Not Done.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Sure, I said it is an hypothetical scenario. Just to make an abstract example and, nevertheless, it's not so hard to find a NPC with 5 in both presence and Persuasion. According to the core book, such a NPC should be one of the most persuasive man/woman in the whole world.<br /> Obviously it woldu be silly to use this kind of NPC to obtain the gola in my example, unless it is a story hook... but for other reasons maybe... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T14:39:47-07:00","id":862196,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862094#post862094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Sure, but are you able to convince a player that abandon all his political power, betray the trust of the Prince, to leave with the NPC and start a new un-life, from zero, elsewehere, just describing a wide smile and the sensation of butterfles in the stomach (Hig presence+persuasion)? I don't think so, while a PNC (or other PC), with Presence 5, Persuasion 5 and Majesty 3-4, could achieve this goal.<br /> It's just an hypotetical situation but it describe something that really could happen in real life. You know, people that leave work and sell everything to satisfy a woman that then abandons him.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's where we differ, then, because I don't think anyone could ever convince you of that, 13-14 dice or not. Not without mind control powers, which that person would have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=862120#post862120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's also important to remember that your experience, no matter how vast you may consider it, is not the total experience of all gamers everywhere. What happens at your table, what the players bring to it, how they react to things, how you plan or don't, is going to be entirely different even from another table where they they follow the same rules. How you or your players would react to things is not how all players would react to things.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that's why this discussion is so interesting to me.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T03:16:04-07:00","id":863093,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Storyteller</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=861624#post861624\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The real problem is because you're tantalizing them with an in game prize, you're tantalizing them with a meta game prize. It's not &quot;Do this thing your character wouldn't do because of a thing they character can directly connect to what they want.&quot; It's &quot;make your character do this thing they wouldn't otherwise do in order to magically learn 1/15 of a new super power.&quot; We kind of went through this before, though, so, we can just agree to disagree, that's ok.<br /> <br /> But, PCs do rely on their tactical knowledge to make smart moves in combats and deal damage. And they rely on their intelligence and ability to parse the system and spend their points/XP wisely to make the most effective characters. Real life skills leak into the game all over the place, so, objecting to this specific one (i.e. being persuasive) is always strange to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that PCs rely on meta-knowledge to make decisions. This is why you offer beats; it simulates that the person's offer is attractive due to superficial reasons (the person speaks very well, they're very good looking, they're in a position of power). It doesn't change what's really on offer, or what's actually being said, but it does change some of the context to better simulate real life.<br /> <br /> For example: People get used all the time. Go to any bar, notice that guys buy drinks for pretty women all the time. Notice how many of these men are used, and notice how many of them get absolutely nothing for their trouble. It doesn't make a lot of objective sense, and that's a perfectly fine excuse for not buying a pretty woman a drink, but there's certainly a temptation despite your better sense saying otherwise.<br /> <br /> It's not unheard of for a good-looking girl to ask you for a drink at a bar, and even if intellectually you know she's just trying to score a free drink from you and you'd be a total sap to buy her one, there's definitely a degree of temptation on offer. You can still say 'no', but you're still tempted to say 'yes'.<br /> <br /> This is what a beat on offer simulates. You're tempted by a charming voice, a pretty face. You're peer pressured.<br /> <br /> But it's the player's decision. Players should always have agency, it's up to them to make the ultimate decision. They can always say 'no', but the temptation can make it interesting out-of-character and in-character in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T05:11:49-07:00","id":863112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863093#post863093\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For example: People get used all the time. Go to any bar, notice that guys buy drinks for pretty women all the time. Notice how many of these men are used, and notice how many of them get absolutely nothing for their trouble. It doesn't make a lot of objective sense, and that's a perfectly fine excuse for not buying a pretty woman a drink, but there's certainly a temptation despite your better sense saying otherwise.</div> </div> </div> </div> Okay, I really have to object to this. Just because someone chooses to buy a woman a drink does not give a man any right to anything else. Its a drink. Period. It is not a payment for any kind of favor. No one is &quot;being used&quot; here. <br /> <br /> If a guy buys me a drink, that's fine, but its entirely his decision. I didn't ask for it, I didn't promise anything in return for it. I owe the drink buyer NOTHING.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T05:40:11-07:00","id":863121,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863112#post863112\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Okay, I really have to object to this. Just because someone chooses to buy a woman a drink does not give a man any right to anything else. Its a drink. Period. It is not a payment for any kind of favor. No one is &quot;being used&quot; here. <br /> <br /> If a guy buys me a drink, that's fine, but its entirely his decision. I didn't ask for it, I didn't promise anything in return for it. I owe the drink buyer NOTHING.</div> </div> </div> </div>This.<br /> <br /> If anything, I'd argue that the idea that you should get something out of buying someone a drink is itself much like using them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":10056,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","user":"ShadowKnight1224","date":". 04-10-2016, 09:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T07:38:58-07:00","id":863147,"title":"","content":"For the persuasion discussion - I actually actively encourage social mechanics and rolling dice for it, and use &quot;good arguments&quot; or &quot;good roleplaying&quot; as a equipment bonus, because being persuasive isn't really about just picking good words. If that was the case, anyone could be persuasive with a good writer preparing their speeches for them and, as you probably can see in real life, even best speech can be delivered in a off putting way, while a persuasive person can buy you with just few words. Choice of words is important, but there are way more important things that are probably best emulated by characters dots and skills, and I don't like ignoring them in favor of &quot;checking my player for persuasiveness&quot;. And I think this is working, because some of my very shy friends are finally starting to play socially &quot;competent&quot; characters, simply because *it's not their social skill that is checked* to succeed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T07:53:55-07:00","id":863151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863147#post863147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the persuasion discussion - I actually actively encourage social mechanics and rolling dice for it, and use &quot;good arguments&quot; or &quot;good roleplaying&quot; as a equipment bonus, because being persuasive isn't really about just picking good words. If that was the case, anyone could be persuasive with a good writer preparing their speeches for them and, as you probably can see in real life, even best speech can be delivered in a off putting way, while a persuasive person can buy you with just few words. Choice of words is important, but there are way more important things that are probably best emulated by characters dots and skills, and I don't like ignoring them in favor of &quot;checking my player for persuasiveness&quot;. And I think this is working, because some of my very shy friends are finally starting to play socially &quot;competent&quot; characters, simply because *it's not their social skill that is checked* to succeed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like your idea of equipment bonus for good arguments...really like <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T08:07:24-07:00","id":863153,"title":"","content":"To provide some context for my mini rant: I don't like focusing too much on the words used in the argument, because it's very reductionist; &quot;message&quot; (and in general, communication between people) is much more complicated than that, and a message is created from many more moving parts than just &quot;what you say&quot;; and a lot of them isn't really included in role playing descriptions. This, in general, leads to a curious situation where you have a very incomplete message, so you intuitively &quot;complete it&quot; by injecting with the missing factors taken from the *player*, not *player's character*. <br /> Like, I can say jokes that make people laugh, and I saw my friend try to say the very same jokes, but completely fail at it, because while he can copy the &quot;words&quot;, he can't copy the other partsof it. Which leads him to be confused why people laugh at my jokes but not at his (and truth is, people laugh at his jokes too, when he says *his* jokes, not *somebody else's* jokes).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T08:54:28-07:00","id":863174,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863147#post863147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And I think this is working, because some of my very shy friends are finally starting to play socially &quot;competent&quot; characters, simply because *it's not their social skill that is checked* to succeed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm going to sound terrible, but if I'm being honest, it has never been fun for the group when my socially incompetent friends try to play socially competent people. And no, this is not because I'm a heartless bitch that won't let them succeed because they can't talk. No, even when compensating and just letting them roll for everything, it doesn't work and it isn't fun. When the group is trying to immerse in their characters, having one guy constantly unable to do that, not talking in character for the most part and just throwing dice at the problem, it's very disorienting. It's also way less effective, even when 100% rolling every social interaction out, because people who are &quot;shy&quot; or whatever the nice word is for not being good at social interaction, don't just have trouble knowing what to say/how to say it, they also have trouble knowing <i>when</i> to talk. It's one thing when the group has a clear goal in mind and a social path to victory is obvious--then they're fine. But people who are good at talking can talk their way out of a lot of situations where it would never even occur to a &quot;shy&quot; person that talking is an option. <br /> <br /> And that's the funny thing about comparing being shy to being out of shape when it comes to roleplaying games. The 98 lb. weakling who can't lift a cat and the 300 pounder whose only exercise is lifting food to his mouth have no trouble at all using their physical skills to solve problems. No matter how unathletic someone is in real life, I've never seen a player whose character can climb, jump, swim, or fight well fail to utilize those abilities when the opportunity arises. Even if one can't breath after running down the hallway, they show no hesitation in parkouring up walls or off roofs, punching their way out of bad situations, taking ridiculous called shots, etc. People who suck at physical activity still understand how physical activity works, they just can't actually do it. But, in my experience, people who suck at social activity suck <i>because </i>they don't understand it.<br /> <br /> It's never been fun for the table when socially awkward people play the party face. Not once, whether I was the GM or not, no matter what special precautions the GM took to help them. However, I have seen a few socially awkward people become no longer awkward from playing roleplaying games where they had to talk to get things done. And I've seen even more that, as themselves, are a social disaster, but immersed in their characters, are smooth talking social masters. <br /> <br /> But this is all based on a different kind of gaming agenda, I believe, a different type of immersive roleplaying, so, if it works for you, that's awesome. It just doesn't for us. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863153#post863153\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To provide some context for my mini rant: I don't like focusing too much on the words used in the argument, because it's very reductionist; &quot;message&quot; (and in general, communication between people) is much more complicated than that, and a message is created from many more moving parts than just &quot;what you say&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>It is, absolutely. But there are times when the message is good enough. If someone offers a legitimately attractive deal, it doesn't matter how much stuttering was used to offer it, as long as the deal came across, the other person might be willing to accept based entirely on the deal. As I've said before, social rolls are the fallback in my games...if your message isn't compelling enough on its own, then you roll for all that non-message stuff and can maybe sway them anyway. But if the deal you present is actually something they'd go along with, it doesn't matter what you roll, they want to do it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863153#post863153\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This, in general, leads to a curious situation where you have a very incomplete message, so you intuitively &quot;complete it&quot; by injecting with the missing factors taken from the *player*, not *player's character*.</div> </div> </div> </div>When immersed in your character, those missing factors are not dramatically different from character to player. That's one of the points of immersing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T10:02:48-07:00","id":863204,"title":"","content":"Throwing dice at the problem is actually a crucial part of learning how to be a good player and ST. It gives you the confidence you need to slowly make progress and push your boundaries because you know that you can always fall back on letting the dice do the work for you, whether that's physically, mentally or socially.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T11:54:30-07:00","id":863247,"title":"","content":"Being immersed doesn't help you with these missing factors, because these missing factors are things like pitch of voice, clothing, touch, posture, distance between people, various communication noises, smell, and other physical (and some of them less physical) signals that blend together and are processed to create your impression of the person you are talking with. And because you lack these elements and there is a person standing right in front of you, good luck convincing your brain that it should consider the text of what that person is saying as totally unrelated to all other signals that person is sending to you. I know I often can't, so I just use dice result as a stand in for these. <br /> And yeah, just because someone isn't very good at talking doesn't mean they are not immersed. My friends are totally immersed when they play, and curiously, they tend to gravitate towards characters that aren't their strongest side; person who makes most social characters is terribly shy, person who plays detective isn't very good at solving mysteries, and so on. And it's definitely not my job (our my friend's who is alternate GM to me) to tell them that &quot;nope, you suck at being socially graceful lady, you fail&quot; or &quot;you are too stupid to solve this shit, better give up on this character&quot;. <br /> <br /> As for message being good enough - try being offered immortality by a stinking, mad-eyed, bearded hobo, and try being offered immortality by a armani-wearing sharp suited devil whose voice makes you feel big in your pants. Their message, their offer might the same, but most people won't take the first dude seriously, even if they say the same thing. I mean, people agreed to electrocute other people just based on the fact that person they are talking to looked like a scientist...people acting in intelectually sound and reasonable way isn't a given. Hell, today we analysed a case where a dude randomly grabbed a gun from police officer's gun holster and shot him, ran away, and then given himself up to other police car that was nearby, because something made his brain take wrong turn while being escorted by the guy to ambulance. What sounds like a rational choice on a paper often isn't the choice picked. Some great business deals can be cancelled simply because the dude on the other side of the table is annoying you due to things you can't put your finger on - something wrong with his teeth, movement of his eyes, way he moves his fingers that subconsciously reminds you of your ex...how do you compensate for all of these factors - that are incredibly hard to include if you are not a professional actor - when deciding on the outcome of the action?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T12:15:13-07:00","id":863255,"title":"","content":"I really miss a thing: why if a player isn't so good at talking can't make a char with expression 5? As a player of about 60kg and lamer can make a PC with athletics 5??<br /> This is a &quot;role playing game&quot; where, for definition, a player interprets a PC totally different from himself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T13:49:36-07:00","id":863309,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863112#post863112\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Okay, I really have to object to this. Just because someone chooses to buy a woman a drink does not give a man any right to anything else. Its a drink. Period. It is not a payment for any kind of favor. No one is &quot;being used&quot; here.<br /> <br /> If a guy buys me a drink, that's fine, but its entirely his decision. I didn't ask for it, I didn't promise anything in return for it. I owe the drink buyer NOTHING.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you missed the point. I am describing someone using seduction to get someone to do something for them. That's it.<br /> <br /> Why is stating that some girls talk gullible guys into buying them drinks objectionable? It happens.<br /> <br /> It might not fit your politically correct sensibilities, but the reality is that some girls do ask for drinks in a bar setting, and gullible guys will buy them a drink under the impression that they have a shot. Why do you think guys buy random girls drinks at bars? Do you think they're doing it because they're an incredibly generous guy who has no ulterior motives whatsoever? Sorry, but that's pretty naive.<br /> <br /> However, I am not saying girls are obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks. I don't really appreciate people putting words in my mouth, so I am just going to politely ask that you don't do that, okay? It's annoying. I am just saying that some people use other people, and people in real life fall over themselves for no objective benefit, simply because some stranger is charismatic / attractive enough to suck that person in.<br /> <br /> This is just one seedy example that fits Vampire perfectly.<br /> <br /> Don't take it personally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T14:46:31-07:00","id":863337,"title":"","content":"The scenario you proposed paints the guy as an innocent victim. Nobody buying drinks for someone they don't know is gullible. He is at the bar in the first place buying drinks only for anonymous strangers who fit his definition of pretty because he is searching for the opportunity to exploit someone's lowered inhibitions (as a side effect of consuming alcohol) in order to make them more likely to consent to sex, a notion they would be less likely to entertain if their decision making capabilities were not impaired. And if the woman frequents bars she's familiar with the fact that some anonymous guy is going to offer to buy her a drink without asking in hopes of getting laid because she now &quot;owes him&quot; her time or she's too drunk to say no, so she's going to try and get something in the mean time for putting up with his unsolicited advances. Nobody is being &quot;used&quot; in that scenario.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5466,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","user":"tsusasi","date":". 04-10-2016, 05:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T14:46:37-07:00","id":863338,"title":"","content":"These guys try to offer drinks for equipment bonus to their seduction rolls. If you are using social manuevering, it's a bribe I guess.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T14:47:56-07:00","id":863339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863309#post863309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you missed the point. I am describing someone using seduction to get someone to do something for them. That's it.</div> </div> </div> </div>You implied that accepting someone's offer to buy a drink while having no intention of giving them anything in return is &quot;using&quot; the person offering the drink.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863309#post863309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why is stating that some girls talk gullible guys into buying them drinks objectionable? It happens.</div> </div> </div> </div>What is objectionable is the implication that the person who accepts the solicitation to buy the other person a drink is somehow being had.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863309#post863309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might not fit your politically correct sensibilities, but the reality is that some girls do ask for drinks in a bar setting, and gullible guys will buy them a drink under the impression that they have a shot.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, that impression is the problem. Unless the woman says &quot;buy me a drink and you'll have a shot at getting into my pants&quot;, then any impression the guy has that he might &quot;have a shot&quot; is something entirely of his own invention. And yet the woman is blamed for &quot;using&quot; or &quot;tricking&quot; him. He's seen as the poor gullible guy who got fooled by the nefarious gold digger instead of the entitled asshole who took it as a given that if he bought a woman a drink, she would owe him something in return. And that's fucked up.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863309#post863309\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why do you think guys buy random girls drinks at bars? Do you think they're doing it because they're an incredibly generous guy who has no ulterior motives whatsoever? Sorry, but that's pretty naive.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, I'm fully aware why they do it. I just don't see the guy's ulterior motives as the woman's fault.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 04-10-2016, 05:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T20:57:24-07:00","id":863468,"title":"","content":"What Tsuasi and Charlaquin said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T22:49:22-07:00","id":863493,"title":"","content":"The guy that sees women as vendor machines where he inputs money (in the form of drinks) in exchange for whatever he thinks he's entitled to is not being used or taken advantage of, save by his own delusions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/872434-shadow-knight-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">My homebrew hub</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10056-shadowknight1224","name":"ShadowKnight1224","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"975"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T05:40:40-07:00","id":863580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863339#post863339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You implied that accepting someone's offer to buy a drink while having no intention of giving them anything in return is &quot;using&quot; the person offering the drink.<br /> <br /> <br /> What is objectionable is the implication that the person who accepts the solicitation to buy the other person a drink is somehow being had.<br /> <br /> <br /> See, that impression is the problem. Unless the woman says &quot;buy me a drink and you'll have a shot at getting into my pants&quot;, then any impression the guy has that he might &quot;have a shot&quot; is something entirely of his own invention. And yet the woman is blamed for &quot;using&quot; or &quot;tricking&quot; him. He's seen as the poor gullible guy who got fooled by the nefarious gold digger instead of the entitled asshole who took it as a given that if he bought a woman a drink, she would owe him something in return. And that's fucked up.<br /> <br /> <br /> Oh, I'm fully aware why they do it. I just don't see the guy's ulterior motives as the woman's fault.</div> </div> </div> </div>No. I described a scenario where girls ask guys for drinks with the intention of scoring free drinks. It happens. I have friends that do it. I have done it myself. It's common. That's just one example of people using charm to score free shit from gullible people. I don't find anything I have written about that objectionable in the slightest. Are you saying people don't use their looks and charm to score free stuff from other people? Are you saying people don't take advantage of idiots? There's nothing wrong with saying that some gullible dupe is being used.<br /> <br /> Personal experiences:<br /> <br /> A drunk friend comes up to me laughing, she tells me 'she promised some guy she'd sleep with them but she really wasn't going to', and that sap kept buying her drinks all night. Was that guy used? Absolutely. My personal experience obviously differs from yours so hopefully more context helps us reach a better understanding. Another example: another person I know gets a mentally handicapped person to drive her around, buy her drinks, clothes, that sort of stuff (apparently they're 'friends', but it's obvious the guy is being used and she's an awful human being for taking advantage of him). Another example: a male friend told another female friend that he loved her to get her to sleep with him (he lied, basically using her for sex). She was hurt, of course.<br /> <br /> However, I have a request if we're going to continue this discussion:<br /> <br /> Can you stop countering things I have never even said? For example, &quot;I just don't see the guy's ulterior motives as the woman's fault.&quot; I never said that. I don't mind clarifying things, but I don't like being misrepresented. I explicitly wrote, &quot;I am not saying girls are obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks.&quot; I noticed you missed that line in your response and will ask that you start presenting my quotes in their entirety and stop misrepresenting what I have written. We're not going to reach any sort of understanding if you're going to draw 'implications' on the fly and counter things that were never said. If you're simply trying to make a point that 'women aren't obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks', then yeah, I agree (already clarified that point in my last post). But it doesn't take away from the fact that some girls do explicitly ask guys for drinks with the intention of scoring free drinks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1592,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","user":"Jack","date":". 04-11-2016, 08:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T07:14:02-07:00","id":863618,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1592_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1592\" data-vbnamecard=\"1592\">Jack</a> , you should have just made it a personal story instead of a story about girls getting boys to buy them drinks. &quot;If I flirt with someone all night, insinuating that I'll let them come home with me, in an effort to just get free drinks - I'm using them.&quot; Done, point made.<br /> <br /> But drawing attention to only the 'girls getting free drinks' scenario isn't the whole story and is problematic considering the growing pains the gaming community is going through for inclusivity. I don't <i>think</i> you meant it that way, but it was a clumsy tell. Pretty boys can manipulate people just as much as pretty girls, and not every relationship is hetero anyway. <br /> <br /> Your general point that Striking Looks confers an advantage that unscrupulous people can use to profit is sound, but it's only hurt by making it a gender specific anecdote. I know you weren't saying that only girls do it, but by including only that aspect, that's how it came across - and it's reasonable for people to attack that girls-owe-guys concept, even though I don't think you intended it.<br /> <br /> No one's saying that women never manipulate people, either. So throwing up further examples of that isn't addressing the core problem - which was your example didn't tell the whole story, and was uncomfortably close to the rhetoric people use to slut-shame or tease-shame women.<br /> <br /> Just acknowledge that wasn't your attention and clarify your greater point without using gender specific terminology.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4047-holy","name":"Holy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4047_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"274"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T09:44:21-07:00","id":863719,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863580#post863580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No. I described a scenario where girls ask guys for drinks with the intention of scoring free drinks. It happens. I have friends that do it. I have done it myself. It's common. That's just one example of people using charm to score free shit from gullible people. I don't find anything I have written about that objectionable in the slightest. Are you saying people don't use their looks and charm to score free stuff from other people? Are you saying people don't take advantage of idiots? There's nothing wrong with saying that some gullible dupe is being used.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're continuing to frame it in terms of the guy being a gullible dupe, which is the problem. People who expect something that was never promised in exchange for something they offered freely are not gullible, they're just entitled.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863580#post863580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personal experiences:<br /> <br /> A drunk friend comes up to me laughing, she tells me 'she promised some guy she'd sleep with them but she really wasn't going to', and that sap kept buying her drinks all night. Was that guy used? Absolutely.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, he was, because your friend actually lied to the guy about giving him something in exchange for the drinks That detail wasn't included in your original post, and it is far from universal. Quite often, the guys assume a promise of sex for drinks when none is given, and <i>that</i> is not being gullible, it's being presumptive.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863580#post863580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My personal experience obviously differs from yours so hopefully more context helps us reach a better understanding. Another example: another person I know gets a mentally handicapped person to drive her around, buy her drinks, clothes, that sort of stuff (apparently they're 'friends', but it's obvious the guy is being used and she's an awful human being for taking advantage of him).</div> </div> </div> </div>In what way is she taking advantage of him here? Again, if she's not actually offering anything in exchange for these services he offers her, there's no deception here. Just one person who likes doing stuff for his friend, and one person who appreciates having stuff done for her.<br /> <br /> I have a similar personal story. When I was in high school, there was a girl I wanted to sleep with. I took her on a lot of dates and bought her a lot of gifts. She never implied in any way that this would lead to sex, and in fact expressed on multiple occasions that I was spending too much money on her and she felt guilty about it. But I insisted, because I thought if I just spent <i>enough</i> money, eventually she would be so greatful she would sleep with me. Needless to say, that never happened and at the time I was pissed about it. Was I used? No, I was acting like an entitled asshat. I thought I &quot;deserved&quot; something that was never promised or even actually offered, it was entirely my own fault. I've grown up since then, but I was pretty much a douche bag back then.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863580#post863580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another example: a male friend told another female friend that he loved her to get her to sleep with him (he lied, basically using her for sex). She was hurt, of course.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, that's him using her because he's actually lying to get something out of her. If he had just asked if she wanted to have sex without claiming to be in love, and she had just assumed he must have loved her if he wanted to have sex with her, that would have been a different story.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jack</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863580#post863580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">However, I have a request if we're going to continue this discussion:<br /> <br /> Can you stop countering things I have never even said? For example, &amp;quot;I just don't see the guy's ulterior motives as the woman's fault.&amp;quot; I never said that. I don't mind clarifying things, but I don't like being misrepresented. I explicitly wrote, &amp;quot;I am not saying girls are obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks.&amp;quot; I noticed you missed that line in your response and will ask that you start presenting my quotes in their entirety and stop misrepresenting what I have written. We're not going to reach any sort of understanding if you're going to draw 'implications' on the fly and counter things that were never said. If you're simply trying to make a point that 'women aren't obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks', then yeah, I agree (already clarified that point in my last post). But it doesn't take away from the fact that some girls do explicitly ask guys for drinks with the intention of scoring free drinks.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, my point isn't that women aren't obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks. My poin is that if person A asks person B to buy them a drink and offers nothing in return for it, person B isn't being used if they do buy person A a drink. Person B is agreeing to something that has no benefit to them, sure, and likely has some underlying motive for why they are doing so. But person A is not taking advantage of person B if person A didn't offer something in exchange for the drink and person B didn't ask for something in exchange for the drink.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-11T13:16:49-07:00","id":863861,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863719#post863719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're continuing to frame it in terms of the guy being a gullible dupe, which is the problem. People who expect something that was never promised in exchange for something they offered freely are not gullible, they're just entitled.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can be gullible and entitled, Charlaquin. They're not mutually exclusive states of being.<br /> <br /> You seem to be hung up on me calling these guys &quot;gullible&quot;, so let me clarify:<br /> <br /> I honestly don't see what's so offensive about saying some guy is gullible for thinking he's going to get sex for buying a girl a drink. Why do you? Also, why is it so unbelievable that some women take advantage of this asinine reasoning by asking for drinks from random guys at crowded bars (considering I gave you an anecdotal example from my own personal experience)? It's not nice, but it happens. They're used. They're not very sympathetic dupes, but they're used nonetheless by people who know they can get something from them for nothing.<br /> <br /> It's a very apt example for Vampire, too. Some gullible kine might think they're going to get laid by some random stud-muffin, but in reality they're just going to be used for their blood. In the context of Vampire, that's another take on a pale girl at a bar asking random strangers for a drink.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863719#post863719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In what way is she taking advantage of him here? Again, if she's not actually offering anything in exchange for these services he offers her, there's no deception here. Just one person who likes doing stuff for his friend, and one person who appreciates having stuff done for her.<br /> <br /> I have a similar personal story. When I was in high school, there was a girl I wanted to sleep with. I took her on a lot of dates and bought her a lot of gifts. She never implied in any way that this would lead to sex, and in fact expressed on multiple occasions that I was spending too much money on her and she felt guilty about it. But I insisted, because I thought if I just spent <i>enough</i> money, eventually she would be so greatful she would sleep with me. Needless to say, that never happened and at the time I was pissed about it. Was I used? No, I was acting like an entitled asshat. I thought I &quot;deserved&quot; something that was never promised or even actually offered, it was entirely my own fault. I've grown up since then, but I was pretty much a douche bag back then.</div> </div> </div> </div>He's mentally handicapped. She gets him to buy her things by reminding him that they're 'friends'. I have seen it personally. It's weird.<br /> <br /> She calls him up to pick her up when she's drunk. Or to drop her off wherever. To buy her drinks when she has no money. Or to buy her clothes when she has no money. She doesn't really consider him a friend, because she says some pretty disparaging things about him behind his back. Even if she genuinely considers him a friend in her own way - and I doubt it -, it's still a pretty toxic relationship. I have seen her ditch him for her 'real friends' quite often. I am not close to him, but my family is close to his, and speaking to his parents, they're very much irked by it all - they actually came to me looking for advice on how to get a restraining order against her. I have personally seen him dropping her off and picking her up from places like some lapdog. Despite not being close to him personally, it's pretty awful to witness. She's clearly taking advantage of him. It definitely leaves a very bad taste in my mouth. Hopefully that further context helps you see my point.<br /> <br /> As for your example, I understand where you're coming from. I agree. You weren't used.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863719#post863719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yep, that's him using her because he's actually lying to get something out of her. If he had just asked if she wanted to have sex without claiming to be in love, and she had just assumed he must have loved her if he wanted to have sex with her, that would have been a different story.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=863719#post863719\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, my point isn't that women aren't obligated to sleep with guys if they buy them drinks. My poin is that if person A asks person B to buy them a drink and offers nothing in return for it, person B isn't being used if they do buy person A a drink. Person B is agreeing to something that has no benefit to them, sure, and likely has some underlying motive for why they are doing so. But person A is not taking advantage of person B if person A didn't offer something in exchange for the drink and person B didn't ask for something in exchange for the drink.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think context is important, but certainly in my experience there are girls who try to mooch free drinks from guys. It's not like these girls are socially ignorant, and taking advantage of a culture of guys who think buying a girl a drink is the usual protocol, yeah, they're using them. I am not describing every girl who gets a drink bought for them; I am speaking about the girls who specifically trawl bars for guys to buy them drinks (assuming you have experience going out to bars, you should know the type I am talking about - it's in the same league as that stereotypical person at a bar who tries to bum smokes from everybody - it's possibly a cultural thing, though).<br /> <br /> edit: in any case, I think we've reached a conclusion to this back-and-forth (even if we may not agree on the finer details). But this conversation is pretty divorced from my actual point, which was to give people beats for following persuasive characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1592,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","user":"Jack","date":". 04-11-2016, 05:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1592-jack","name":"Jack","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1592_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"329"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-13T06:35:28-07:00","id":864884,"title":"","content":"OKAY! Time for a new question unrelated to anything before. Kind of. <br /> <br /> <br /> So, in 1e, Persuasion was pretty much an all-important skill for most vampires to cultivate. At least, that was my experience. That skill found its way into a lot of Discipline powers, served as the key skill seduction, and was my go-to skill for getting another person to do something. Not surprising, really, since that's the name of of the skill - Persuasion. <br /> <br /> However, in 2e, we now use a general Social Maneuvering system for convincing others of doing something. And you can pretty much use any skill. In fact, we have three Maneuvering Styles - Fast Talk, Etiquette, and Enchanting Performance (CtL only). While you can use almost any skill with Fast Talk, it primarily uses Subterfuge. Likewise, Etiquette and Enchanting Performance use Socialize and Expression. Persuasion isn't as necessary to, well, persuade anyone anymore. Or seduce them - I've acomplished most seductions using Maneuver Styles and other social merits than relying on the Persuasion skill. In Vampire specifically, Persuasion is actually a pretty rare skill to use for powers. Only three devotions, two of which tend to rely on a person's agreement in the first place, and the last is Summoning. I feel like there's been a very deliberate step away from Persuasion as a skill in VtR. Which is actually thematic, really, with the focus on mind-control powers. You don't convince people, you tell them, and they follow. <br /> <br /> So, here's my question - how much use is Persuasion seeing in other people's chronicles? Do you still feel like its a pretty common skill to use, or have others been taking more of the stage lately? <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-13T07:32:23-07:00","id":864923,"title":"","content":"I allow Persuasion to be used without penalties in situations where other skills might suffer them. For example, Intimidation requires target to be able to fear, and if they are for some reason resilent to the way you are trying to control them, you might get extra penalties. Empathy needs something to work with, and without it, it's pretty impotent. I would, for example, penalize using Empathy on a low-humanity Vampire, and probably flat out downgrade result by one step against non-relatable beings; so when trying to Empathize with Strix or a Spirit, Failure is Ex!Failure, Success is Failure, and Ex!Success is just Success.<br /> In case of Persuasion, none of these would bother the Persuader. Persuasion is the only one skill that lacks Information Gathering Powers (Intimidation has in built Interrogation feature, Empathy is Social Detective, and Subterfuge is perfect for spotting lies), so I imagine that it should compensate for that with sheer versatility and ability to be almost always useful when you just simply want to make someone change their mind.<br /> <br /> A good way to make Persuasion more interesting is to give it cool equipment options for having high dots.<br /> <br /> EDIT<br /> I'm also a fan of pairing Mental Skills with Social Attributes, and vice versa, so if my player wonders &quot;what could make a good offer for him&quot;, I would suggest:<br /> a) Intelligence (or Wits, depending which is better for the character) + Persuasion<br /> b) Manipulation + Politics<br /> c) Intelligence (or Wits) + Empathy, at a penalty if the target wasn't probed<br /> which, again, makes Persuasion and other skills more valuable to have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","user":"WHW","date":". 04-13-2016, 10:36 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-13T12:14:16-07:00","id":865050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=864884#post864884\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, here's my question - how much use is Persuasion seeing in other people's chronicles? Do you still feel like its a pretty common skill to use, or have others been taking more of the stage lately?</div> </div> </div> </div>So, first, I would say that my experience didn't match yours in 1e. Persuasion was not, say, rare (like expression rolls outside of discipline powers rare), but the most used social skills were Subterfuge and Streetwise, for sure, and so far, still are. Lying/fast talk (and I thought fast talk was subterfuge in 1e, too) and dealing with the underground/homeless/etc. is very common for fresh embraces. After that, I would say Empathy even still beat Persuasion in 1e, and in 2e, Socialize does, too.<br /> <br /> Persuasion is a weird skill because it's what you use to get someone to do something or change their mind about something, but, I mean, how often does that <i>really</i> come up? And not just in a Vampire game where the people who want to change your mind most will just mind control you. In real life, even, how often are you actually trying to convince someone of something? Most of the time, you're just trying to be pleasant and likable (socialize) or lying (subterfuge) or figuring something out about them (empathy). Most real &quot;persuasion&quot; isn't &quot;change your mind and do what I want&quot; so much as &quot;come on, we're friends, right? you like me, right?&quot; And yeah, that's socialize (to be genuinely likable) or subterfuge (to fake it) or empathy (to figure out what they actually like or to sympathize with them on what's bugging them). Then, the person is basically going to do what you want on their own because they like you. I just find it rare that someone is actually presenting a real argument or whatever to change someone's mind.<br /> <br /> Does that happen often in games? It doesn't in mine, though I wonder if that's normal or just because of the people I play with or how situations are presented or what.<br /> <br /> I will add the minor disclaimer that I do handle social rolls in my games differently. We don't use the doors system, and we only rarely even roll (only if straight roleplaying fails to provide believable results or the player asks to roll), but even analyzing past interactions, only very, very rarely was persuading the thing that was going on in a social scene.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/860359-persuasion-on-players?p=864923#post864923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">EDIT<br /> I'm also a fan of pairing Mental Skills with Social Attributes, and vice versa, so if my player wonders &quot;what could make a good offer for him&quot;, I would suggest:<br /> a) Intelligence (or Wits, depending which is better for the character) + Persuasion<br /> b) Manipulation + Politics<br /> c) Intelligence (or Wits) + Empathy, at a penalty if the target wasn't probed<br /> which, again, makes Persuasion and other skills more valuable to have.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is really good advice, honestly, but I'd probably suggest Wits/Int + Politics over Manipulation, since to me, Manipulation is an active thing--not a knowing stuff or even figuring stuff out thing. Politics is a skill I like, but every time I want to use it as a player, the GM basically ignores/forgets about it, and every time I want to use it as a GM, no player has taken it, so, I feel like a jerk calling for rolls of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2698-the-storyteller","name":"The Storyteller","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}